; R7RS 6.2.6 integer division with an INEXACT operand — exactness contagion
; plus the two sign conventions:
;   modulo    -> result has the sign of the DIVISOR   (floored remainder)
;   remainder -> result has the sign of the DIVIDEND  (truncated remainder)
; An integral flonum IS an integer per R7RS ((integer? 7.0) is #t), so every
; line below must agree with its exact-integer counterpart in
; corpus/26_int_division.esk.
;
; Native was the wrong side here (found/float_remainder_modulo_native.esk):
; `remainder` called the C library's remainder(), which is IEEE-754
; round-to-NEAREST remainder — a different function — and `modulo` had no
; flonum path at all, so both operands went through the int64 unpack and it
; returned the SRem of two IEEE-754 bit patterns.
;
; NOT gated here (the VM is the wrong side, still filed in found/):
;   (remainder 5.5 2.0)      — VM truncates the operands to integers and
;                              answers 1; native answers 1.5.
;   (modulo <flonum> 0.0)    — divisor-is-zero handling.
(display (modulo 5.5 2.0)) (newline)
(display (remainder 7.0 2.0)) (newline)
(display (modulo 7.0 2.0)) (newline)
(display (remainder -7.0 3.0)) (newline)
(display (modulo -7.0 3.0)) (newline)
(display (remainder 7.0 -3.0)) (newline)
(display (modulo 7.0 -3.0)) (newline)
(display (remainder -7.0 -3.0)) (newline)
(display (modulo -7.0 -3.0)) (newline)
(display (modulo 5 2.0)) (newline)
(display (remainder 5 2.0)) (newline)
(display (modulo 5.0 2)) (newline)
(display (remainder 5.0 2)) (newline)
